<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gunpowder Plot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1605">1605</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Gunpowder Treason Plot, Jesuit Treason</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">Gunpowder</a> Plot of 1605 was an unsuccessful attempt by a group of English Catholics, led by Robert Catesby, to kill King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> and I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The plan was to blow up the House of Lords on 5 November 1605, as the prelude to a revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> in which the king's nine-year-old daughter, Princess Elizabeth, was to be installed as head of state. After an anonymous letter of warning reached William Parker, 4th Baron Monteagle, a search on 4 November found Guy Fawkes guarding 36 barrels of gunpowder. Catesby was shot dead at Holbeche House; eight surviving conspirators, including Fawkes, were convicted in January 1606 and sentenced to be hanged, drawn and quartered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gunpowder-plot.jpg" alt="Gunpowder Plot" width="330" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (printed for P. Brooksby, I. Deacon, I. Blare, I. Back.) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GunpowderPlot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Between 1532 and 1540 King Henry VIII took control of the English Church from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. After Elizabeth died on 24 March 1603, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> succeeded her, swearing that he would not persecute any who would be quiet and outwardly obey the law, and some Catholics hoped for relief.</p><p>None came, and other plots followed in 1603. In the Bye Plot the priests William Watson and William Clark planned to kidnap James and hold him in the Tower until he was more tolerant, and in the Main Plot Lord Cobham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh">Walter Raleigh</a> and others planned to replace him with Arbella Stuart. Watson and Clark were executed, and James reprieved the others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Robert Catesby, wounded and captured in the Essex Rebellion of 1601 and fined 4,000 marks, met Thomas Wintour, John Wright, Guy Fawkes and Thomas Percy on 20 May 1604, probably at the Duck and Drake Inn off the Strand, and the five swore secrecy on a prayer book. Fawkes, using the name John Johnson, took charge of a house near the Prince's Chamber, and on 25 March 1605 the plotters bought the lease of an undercroft directly beneath the House of Lords.</p><p>An anonymous letter of warning sent on 26 October to the Catholic Lord Monteagle was shown to the king on 1 November, and he took the word blow to hint at some stratagem of fire and powder. After Fawkes was seized guarding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> on 4 November he was questioned at the Tower on James's orders, which allowed the gentler tortures first, and confessed on 7 November. Most of the conspirators fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and at Holbeche House the fugitives spread damp gunpowder before the fire, and a spark set it alight and burned Catesby and others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Sir Edward Coke interrogated the prisoners over about ten weeks. The principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Henry Garnet, was convicted of high treason and put to death, although doubt has been cast on how much he knew, since he learned of the plot through confession. On 30 January 1606 four of the conspirators were hanged, drawn and quartered, and Thomas Wintour, Ambrose Rookwood, Robert Keyes and Fawkes met the same sentence the next day, although Fawkes broke his neck jumping from the gallows.</p><p>The Popish Recusants Act of 1606 returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to the Elizabethan system of fines and introduced an Oath of Allegiance. The Observance of 5th November Act 1605 made commemorative services annual until 1859, and bonfires and fireworks gave rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-fawkes-night">Bonfire Night</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gunpowder Plot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
